<GuideLayout title="Pantry Bin Planner" description="Plan pantry containers around the real shelf opening, product access and the foods you use most often." canonicalPath="/guides/pantry-bin-planner/" eyebrow="Pantry guide">
  <p>Pantry organization works best when the containers fit both the shelf and the routine. A visually dense layout can become frustrating when breakfast items are trapped behind occasional baking supplies or when a tall bin cannot be lifted past the shelf above.</p>

  <h2>Measure each shelf separately</h2>
  <p>Do not assume every shelf in the same cabinet has identical usable dimensions. Shelf pins, face frames, hinges and a fixed top rail can change the clear opening. Record the narrowest width, usable height and closing depth for each level.</p>

  <h2>Group items by access frequency</h2>
  <p>Before entering container dimensions, decide which bins need easy access. Good candidates include snacks, breakfast foods, coffee supplies and daily cooking staples. Mark those objects as easy-reach priorities so the planner can favor them in the Easy Access and Balanced results.</p>

  <h2>Use outside dimensions, not capacity labels</h2>
  <p>A “10 litre” bin does not have a standard footprint. Tapered sides, handles and lids can make two containers with the same stated capacity occupy different shelf space. Enter the widest external width, height and depth.</p>

  <h2>Reserve lifting and pulling space</h2>
  <p>Open-top bins may only need enough room for a hand grip. Lidded or stackable bins may need vertical lift. Deep shelves often need front clearance so a container can tilt or slide without catching the frame.</p>

  <h2>Mixing sizes can reduce wasted strips</h2>
  <p>Identical bins create a calm appearance, but a narrow leftover strip may be unusable. A smaller container can sometimes use that strip for packets, spices or refill bags. Compare a uniform layout with a mixed layout rather than assuming one product size is always best.</p>

  <h2>A practical ShelfSketch workflow</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Create one project for one pantry shelf.</li>
    <li>Enter the clear inside dimensions and a modest gap.</li>
    <li>Add only containers you are genuinely considering.</li>
    <li>Set quantities and rotation permissions.</li>
    <li>Mark everyday bins for easy access.</li>
    <li>Generate all three layouts.</li>
    <li>Export the preferred result and take it to the shop.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Plan labels after the physical layout</h2>
  <p>Labels improve recognition, but they should not drive the first fit calculation. First confirm that the containers fit and remain removable. Then decide whether the front face is visible enough for labels or whether top labels are more practical.</p>

  <h2>Common pantry mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>measuring shelf depth but forgetting the closed door;</li>
    <li>using internal bin dimensions from a product listing;</li>
    <li>buying a full set before testing one physical unit;</li>
    <li>stacking frequently used containers under heavy or awkward items;</li>
    <li>using an exact fit with no finger clearance.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="notice"><strong>Food safety remains separate.</strong><br/>ShelfSketch plans physical placement only. It does not assess food storage conditions, temperature, allergens or expiration dates.</div>
